    <body>
      <sec id="article-31431.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Primary immunodeficiencies are a diverse group of more than 450 genetic disorders that impair innate and adaptive immunity, leading to recurrent infections, autoimmune disease, lymphoproliferation, inflammatory complications, and an increased risk of malignancy. This course reviews the primary 7 X-linked conditions among them that are particularly severe, often presenting in early childhood with characteristic infection patterns, immune dysfunction, and multisystem involvement.&#x000a0;Targeted immunologic evaluation and genetic confirmation, which remain essential for reducing morbidity and enabling timely curative interventions such as hematopoietic cell transplantation or emerging gene-based therapies, are also discussed.</p>
        <p>This activity provides an overview of X-linked primary immunodeficiencies, emphasizing clinical presentation, diagnostic strategies, and evidence-based management. Participants will also gain an understanding of the early warning signs, interpretation of laboratory and genetic findings, and coordination of interprofessional care for affected patients.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying X-linked immunodeficiencies, performing the recommended evaluation, selecting antimicrobial prophylaxis and immunoglobulin replacement, determining immunomodulatory therapy, integrating transplant considerations, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition to&#x000a0;improve long-term outcomes for patients with complex immune disorders.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate the clinical presentations associated with each of the major X-linked immunodeficiency syndromes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate patients with suspected X-linked immunodeficiencies using appropriate laboratory investigations to guide treatment decisions.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based management strategies for X-linked immunodeficienciesvbased on current clinical guidelines.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate care across interprofessional teams to optimize outcomes for patients with X-linked immunodeficiencies.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=31431&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=31431">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Primary immunodeficiencies are a heterogeneous group of inherited disorders affecting the immune system, with more than 450 distinct genetic defects identified to date.&#x000a0;Patients with an X-linked immunodeficiency typically present with frequent and recurrent infections alongside immune dysregulation manifesting as autoimmunity, lymphoproliferation, granuloma formation, chronic inflammatory diseases, and increased susceptibility to malignancies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Among this group of disorders, 7 major X-linked immunodeficiency disorders have been well-characterized: X-linked chronic granulomatous disease (X-CGD), X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome, X-linked lymphoproliferative syndrome (XLP), Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS), X-linked severe combined immunodeficiency (X-SCID), X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA), and IPEX (Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked) syndrome. These conditions result in cellular or humoral immune deficiency, leading to serious infections and increased morbidity and mortality from early life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r2">[2]</xref> Identifying carriers remains challenging in the absence of family history, despite advances in genetic testing.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Chronic Granulomatous Disease&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>CGD is a genetically heterogeneous disorder characterized by recurrent life-threatening bacterial and fungal infections with granuloma formation. X-linked CGD results from mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">CYBB</italic> gene encoding gp91phox, accounting for approximately 70% of CGD cases. These mutations disrupt the NADPH oxidase complex, impairing phagocyte function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Hyper-IgM Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>This syndrome results from mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">CD40LG</italic> gene encoding CD40 ligand, impairing class-switch recombination and characterized by normal or elevated IgM levels with deficient IgG and IgA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r4">[4]</xref> CD40L-CD40 interactions are critical for B-cell immunoglobulin isotype switching and effective T-cell responses.</p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Lymphoproliferative Syndrome&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>XLP type 1 results from mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">SH2D1A</italic> gene encoding signaling lymphocyte activation molecule (SLAM)-associated protein (SAP).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r5">[5]</xref> These mutations lead to dysregulated immune responses to Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection, manifesting as fulminant infectious mononucleosis, dysgammaglobulinemia, and B-cell lymphoma.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>WAS results from mutations in the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">WAS</italic>&#x000a0;gene encoding WASp, a critical regulator of actin cytoskeleton remodeling in hematopoietic cells.&#x000a0;The disease presents with variable severity depending on residual WASp expression and function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Severe Combined Immunodeficiency</bold>
</p>
        <p>X-SCID results from mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">IL2RG</italic> gene encoding the common gamma chain cytokine receptor subunit, which is essential for multiple interleukin signaling pathways. This defect causes severe disruption of lymphocyte development, resulting in the absence of T and NK cells and nonfunctional B cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Agammaglobulinemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>XLA&#x000a0;is caused by mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">BTK</italic> gene encoding Bruton tyrosine kinase. <italic toggle="yes">BTK</italic> is essential for B-cell development, and its absence arrests B-cell maturation at the pre-B cell stage, resulting in profound hypogammaglobulinemia and antibody deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>IPEX Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>IPEX results from mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">FOXP3</italic> gene, a transcription factor crucial for regulatory T cell (Treg) development and function. Over 70 distinct <italic toggle="yes">FOXP3</italic> mutations have been identified, leading to loss of Treg-mediated immune suppression and resulting in severe multiorgan autoimmunity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-31431.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 690px;" border="5" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Pause and Reflect</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>A 9-month-old male infant presents with his third episode of pneumonia requiring hospitalization. His maternal uncle died at the age of 3 from recurrent infections. Laboratory testing reveals absent B cells (CD19+ &#x0003c;1%), IgG 45 mg/dL (reference range, 231-762 mg/dL), IgA undetectable, and IgM &#x0003c;5 mg/dL.</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Which X-linked immunodeficiency is most likely based on the immunologic profile?</p></list-item><list-item><p>What genetic test should be ordered to confirm the diagnosis?</p></list-item><list-item><p>What immediate interventions are needed while awaiting genetic results?</p></list-item><list-item><p>What is the definitive treatment for this condition?</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>X-linked primary immunodeficiencies are individually rare but collectively represent an important subset of monogenic immune disorders, with varying prevalence across the population (see&#x000a0;<bold>Table:</bold>&#x000a0;Prevalence of&#x000a0;X-Linked Immunodeficiency Disorders). CGD has an estimated prevalence of approximately 1 in 200,000 to 250,000 live births, with most patients diagnosed before age 5; X-linked forms typically present earlier than autosomal recessive variants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;XLP syndrome affects an estimated 1 to 3 males per million births.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Furthermore, WAS occurs in approximately 1 in 100,000 live births.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>SCID is the most common SCID subtype, accounting for 30% to 40% of all SCID cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;XLA has an estimated incidence of 1 in 190,000 male births based on a report on the United States registry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome is rarer, with an estimated incidence of approximately 1 per&#x000a0;1 million live births. The median age at diagnosis is 1 to 2 years, and more than 90% of affected males become symptomatic by age 4.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The true prevalence of IPEX syndrome remains uncertain due to phenotypic variability and high early mortality in untreated infants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Table</bold>. Prevalence of&#x000a0;X-Linked Immunodeficiency Disorders</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-31431.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="width: 477.34375px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
