        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>X-Chronic Granulomatous Disease&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>In X-linked CGD, <italic toggle="yes">gp91phox</italic> mutations cause absent or severely impaired oxidative burst, preventing phagocytes from generating reactive oxygen species via the NADPH oxidase system and leading to defective clearance of catalase-positive organisms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r3">[3]</xref> This leads to granuloma formation and chronic inflammation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Hyper-IgM</bold>
</p>
        <p>X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome results from mutations in <italic toggle="yes">CD40L</italic>, which disrupt CD40L&#x02013;CD40 interactions critical for immunoglobulin class-switch recombination. This impairs B-cell isotype switching and interferes with T-cell interactions with dendritic cells and macrophages, leading to a combined immunodeficiency phenotype.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Lymphoproliferative Syndrome&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>SLAM-SAP regulates signaling through SLAM family receptors in T lymphocytes, natural killer cells, and natural killer T cells. SAP deficiency impairs cytotoxicity, disrupts germinal center formation, and prevents effective control of EBV infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>In Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, the Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome protein (WASp) is defective or nonfunctional. WASp dysfunction compromises actin polymerization, impairing cytoskeletal dynamics in hematopoietic cells, which disrupts immune cell migration, phagocytosis, immune synapse formation, and platelet production. WASp deficiency disrupts T-cell priming, B-cell responses, and thrombocytopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (X-SCID)</bold>
</p>
        <p>X-SCID is caused by <italic toggle="yes">IL2RG</italic>&#x000a0;mutations affecting the common gamma chain shared by multiple cytokine receptors. Mutations in&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">IL2RG</italic>&#x000a0;disrupt signaling required for lymphocyte development, leading to absent T cells and natural killer cells, as well as nonfunctional B cells, which define&#x000a0;the characteristic T-B+NK-phenotype.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Agammaglobulinemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) is essential for B-cell receptor signaling and B-cell maturation. BTK deficiency arrests B-cell development at the pre-B cell stage, leading to the absence of mature B cells, plasma cells, and immunoglobulin production, resulting in a profound humoral immunodeficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked (IPEX) syndrome </bold>
</p>
        <p>IPEX syndrome results from mutations in the <italic toggle="yes">FOXP3</italic> gene, which encodes the master transcription factor for Treg development and function. <italic toggle="yes">FOXP3</italic> mutations impair Treg suppressive activity, leading to loss of immune homeostasis and widespread autoimmunity, which underlie the characteristic clinical features of enteropathy, endocrinopathies, and dermatitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>X-linked primary immunodeficiencies often present in early childhood with recurrent infections, failure to thrive, and autoimmune manifestations. Careful attention to infection patterns, growth parameters, and physical examination findings is critical for early recognition. History should focus on the type, frequency, and severity of infections, family history of immunodeficiency, and autoimmune or inflammatory complications. Physical examination may reveal lymphoid tissue abnormalities, hepatosplenomegaly, eczema, or bleeding diatheses, depending on the specific disorder. Recognition of characteristic patterns facilitates targeted diagnostic testing and early intervention.</p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Chronic Granulomatous Disease</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients present with recurrent infections caused by catalase-positive organisms (eg, <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus, Serratia, Burkholderia, Aspergillus</italic>) involving the lungs, skin, lymph nodes, and liver.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r14">[14]</xref> Granulomatous inflammation can cause complications in the gastrointestinal and genitourinary tracts, and CGD-associated colitis occurs in 30% to 40% of patients. Physical examination may reveal hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Hyper-IgM</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients develop recurrent upper and lower respiratory tract infections with encapsulated bacteria, as well as opportunistic infections including <italic toggle="yes">Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, Cryptosporidium,</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Histoplasma</italic>. An increased risk of hepatobiliary disease (eg, sclerosing cholangitis, cholangiocarcinoma) and lymphoma is also present in these patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Lymphoproliferative Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>XLP presents as a classic triad including fulminant infectious mononucleosis, dysgammaglobulinemia, and B-cell lymphoproliferative disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r18">[18]</xref> Patients may also develop hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, aplastic anemia, and lymphocytic vasculitis. Laboratory findings include low IgG, often elevated IgM, and reduced natural killer cell cytotoxicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Classic&#x000a0;WAS presents with the triad of microthrombocytopenia, eczema, and recurrent infections. Severity varies with residual WASp expression. Approximately 50% of patients develop autoimmune complications, including hemolytic anemia, vasculitis, and inflammatory bowel disease. Hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy may also be observed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Severe Combined Immunodeficiency</bold>
</p>
        <p>X-linked SCID is a life-threatening primary immunodeficiency that typically presents in early infancy. X-SCID can now be detected through newborn screening programs, which measure T-cell receptor excision circles (TRECs), allowing early diagnosis before the onset of infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r10">[10]</xref> Affected infants often have a family history of immunodeficiency or early infant deaths. Clinical suspicion arises in the presence of recurrent, severe, or opportunistic infections, chronic diarrhea, failure to thrive, and oral candidiasis. Physical examination may reveal failure to thrive, lymphoid hypoplasia, and absence or reduction of tonsillar and lymphoid tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-linked agammaglobulinemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients typically present after 6 months of age as maternal antibodies wane. Recurrent sinopulmonary infections with encapsulated bacteria are characteristic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with&#x000a0;XLA typically have underdeveloped lymphoid tissues, including the spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, and Peyer&#x02019;s patches, with tonsils often small to absent and lymph nodes non-palpable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked (IPEX) syndrome </bold>
</p>
        <p>Classic presentation includes early-onset type 1 diabetes, severe enteropathy with chronic diarrhea, and eczematous dermatitis. Additional manifestations include autoimmune cytopenias, thyroiditis, nephropathy, and hepatitis. Growth retardation is common. Atypical presentations with late onset or single-organ involvement are increasingly recognized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The evaluation of X-linked primary immunodeficiencies requires a systematic approach that begins with baseline laboratory studies and then expands to targeted immunologic and genetic testing based on clinical suspicion. Initial screening typically includes a complete blood count with differential, comprehensive metabolic panel, erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), C-reactive protein (CRP), and quantitative serum immunoglobulins (IgG, IgA, IgM, IgE). Assessment of vaccine-specific antibody titers (eg, tetanus, diphtheria, and pneumococcal polysaccharide) is essential for evaluating functional humoral immunity.</p>
        <p>Further evaluation relies on flow cytometry-based analysis of lymphocyte subsets (T, B, NK cells), neutrophil oxidative burst assays, and protein expression studies (eg, CD40L, WASp, SAP, CD132). These targeted tests help distinguish between defects of neutrophil function, B-cell maturation, T-cell development, or regulatory T-cell pathways.</p>
        <p>When an immunologic defect is identified, molecular confirmation with gene sequencing is required for definitive diagnosis, genetic counseling, and family screening. In families with known mutations, prenatal or newborn screening is recommended, particularly for disorders detectable via TREC or neutrophil function testing.</p>
        <p>
<bold>
<bold>X-Chronic Granulomatous Disease</bold>
</bold>
</p>
        <p>In patients with CGD, laboratory evaluation may show elevated inflammatory markers, eg, ESR and CRP, hypergammaglobulinemia, and anemia of chronic disease. Hypoalbuminemia is often present in individuals with gastrointestinal involvement. Historically, the nitroblue tetrazolium test was used to assess neutrophil superoxide production; however, it has been largely replaced since the late 1990s by the dihydrorhodamine flow cytometric assay, which is now the gold standard for diagnosing chronic granulomatous disease. Abnormalities detected on complementary DNA analysis should be confirmed with genomic sequencing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Hyper-IgM</bold>
</p>
        <p>Laboratory evaluation shows low IgG, IgA, and IgE with normal or elevated IgM. B-cell counts are normal, but switched memory B cells are significantly reduced. Flow cytometry demonstrates the absence of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">CD40L</italic> expression on activated T cells. Genetic sequencing confirms <italic toggle="yes">CD40LG</italic> mutations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Lymphoproliferative Syndrome&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evaluation of suspected XLP begins with a detailed history of severe or atypical EBV infection, fulminant infectious mononucleosis, hypogammaglobulinemia, or unexplained cytopenias. Diagnosis requires demonstration of absent or markedly reduced SAP protein expression on flow cytometry or identification of <italic toggle="yes">SH2D1A</italic> mutations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;EBV testing is essential, as infection frequently triggers clinical manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r26">[26]</xref> Immunologic evaluation may reveal hypogammaglobulinemia and reduced numbers of natural killer T cells, which are characteristic laboratory abnormalities in affected males.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evaluation of WAS&#x000a0;should be considered in male infants or children who present with early-onset bleeding, petechiae, eczema, or recurrent infections. Initial testing shows thrombocytopenia with characteristically small platelets, a hallmark of the disorder. Immunologic studies often reveal elevated IgE and IgA levels, low IgM, and eosinophilia. Definitive diagnosis is established by demonstrating absent or reduced WASp expression on flow cytometry or identifying a pathogenic <italic toggle="yes">WAS</italic> gene mutation through molecular testing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Severe Combined Immunodeficiency</bold>
</p>
        <p>X-linked SCID is most often detected through newborn screening programs&#x000a0;using TRECs. TRECs detect infants with absent or extremely low thymic T-cell output.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Typical immunologic findings include profound lymphopenia, absent CD3+ T cells, normal or elevated B-cell counts, and absent or markedly reduced natural killer cells, consistent with the characteristic T-B+NK- phenotype.&#x000a0;Diagnostic confirmation is made through genetic testing, identifying <italic toggle="yes">IL2RG</italic> mutations or by demonstrating absent CD132 (common &#x003b3;-chain) expression on flow cytometry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Agammaglobulinemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evaluation for XLA&#x000a0;should be initiated in young children presenting with recurrent bacterial infections, especially of the sinopulmonary tract, after 6 months of age, when maternally derived antibodies wane. The diagnosis is based on profound hypogammaglobulinemia affecting all isotypes, absent or severely reduced B cells (&#x0003c;2%), and absent plasma cells on tissue biopsy. Vaccine antibody titers are typically low or undetectable, reflecting impaired humoral immunity. Confirmation is achieved through <italic toggle="yes">BTK</italic> gene sequencing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked (IPEX) syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evaluation for IPEX syndrome should be considered in children with early-onset severe autoimmunity, particularly chronic enteropathy, neonatal-onset type 1 diabetes, or eczema. Diagnosis requires identification of pathogenic <italic toggle="yes">FOXP3</italic> mutations. Immunologic studies may show reduced or absent FOXP3+ regulatory T cells, elevated IgE, and eosinophilia. Clinical suspicion should be high in patients with multiple autoimmune cytopenias or multisystem autoimmunity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r30">[30]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of X-linked primary immunodeficiencies involves a combination of preventive, supportive, and definitive curative therapies. Key principles include early recognition and treatment of infections, lifelong antimicrobial prophylaxis where indicated, immunoglobulin replacement therapy for humoral defects, and management of immune dysregulation or inflammatory complications. Definitive therapy often relies on hematopoietic cell transplantation, which can restore immune function and reduce long-term morbidity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r31">[31]</xref> Adjunctive treatments, including immunomodulatory therapy, gene therapy, and targeted biologics, are increasingly used based on disease severity, age at diagnosis, and organ involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Chronic Granulomatous Disease</bold>
</p>
        <p>Management of CGD focuses on preventing infections, controlling inflammatory complications, and curative therapy through hematopoietic cell transplantation.&#x000a0;Lifelong antibacterial and antifungal prophylaxis is recommended, with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and itraconazole. Interferon-gamma may be added as adjunctive therapy to enhance neutrophil function and reduce the frequency of severe infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Inflammatory complications, including granuloma formation and colitis, are managed with corticosteroids for acute flares. Steroid-sparing agents,&#x000a0;eg, azathioprine or sulfasalazine, may be used to minimize long-term steroid toxicity. Allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplantation is the only curative option and is ideally performed early, before irreversible organ damage occurs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r33">[33]</xref> Gene therapy with lentiviral vectors is an emerging approach showing promising early results for patients without suitable donors or with refractory disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Hyper-IgM</bold>
</p>
        <p>X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome management includes prevention of Pneumocystis jirovecii infections with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, and monitoring for liver disease and cryptosporidial infection. If <italic toggle="yes">Cryptosporidium</italic> or microsporidia infection is identified, affected patients should be treated promptly with azithromycin or nitazoxanide. Supportive therapy with lifelong immunoglobulin replacement is essential to prevent bacterial infections. Definitive and curative therapy is hematopoietic cell transplantation, ideally performed before significant liver disease develops.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Lymphoproliferative Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Treatment strategies in XLP&#x000a0;syndrome aim to control acute EBV-related complications with&#x000a0;antiviral therapy and immunoglobulin replacement therapy. Rituximab may be life-saving in patients who develop EBV-associated hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r35">[35]</xref> Hematopoietic cell transplantation is the only definitive curative treatment and should ideally be performed before EBV exposure to prevent fulminant infection and long-term complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Early identification and prompt treatment of bleeding complications and recurrent infections, along with prophylactic antimicrobials and platelet transfusions, is essential to reduce morbidity and improve outcomes. Conservative measures include immunoglobulin replacement and antimicrobial prophylaxis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r36">[36]</xref> Hematopoietic cell transplantation&#x000a0;is curative, with the best outcomes when performed early.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r37">[37]</xref> Lentiviral gene therapy has also demonstrated sustained benefits, including resolution of bleeding, infections, and eczema over long-term follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked Severe Combined Immunodeficiency</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients with X-linked SCID&#x000a0;are highly susceptible to severe infections early in life, making prompt diagnosis and protective measures critical.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r39">[39]</xref> Supportive therapy includes aggressive infection prophylaxis and avoidance of live vaccines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;Long-term data demonstrate sustained immune function, though most patients require immunoglobulin replacement due to incomplete B-cell reconstitution.</p>
        <p>
<bold>X-Linked agammaglobulinemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Boys with X-linked agammaglobulinemia are prone to recurrent bacterial infections due to profound humoral immunodeficiency, making early recognition and immunologic support critical.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r41">[41]</xref> Lifelong immunoglobulin replacement (IVIG or SCIG) is essential, with a target trough IgG level of 500 to 800 mg/dL. Prompt antibiotic therapy is required for bacterial infections. Despite adequate therapy, chronic respiratory complications, including bronchiectasis, remain common, highlighting the need for ongoing monitoring and early intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked (IPEX) syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Therapeutic management in IPEX syndrome focuses on rapid control of immune dysregulation and timely progression to curative therapy.&#x000a0;Initial treatment relies on immunosuppression with corticosteroids, calcineurin inhibitors, or mTOR inhibitors, with emerging biologics, eg, abatacept, serving as effective bridging options.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r43">[43]</xref> Hematopoietic cell transplantation is the only curative treatment and offers the best outcomes when performed before significant organ damage; reduced-intensity conditioning has expanded eligibility to high-risk patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r44">[44]</xref> Supportive care, including nutritional optimization and management of endocrinopathies, remains essential throughout the disease course.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-31431.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 690px;" border="5" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Pause and Reflect</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>A 2-year-old boy with known X-SCID underwent matched sibling&#x000a0;hematopoietic cell transplantation 18 months ago. He achieved full T-cell engraftment but has persistent hypogammaglobulinemia (IgG 285 mg/dL) and continues to experience recurrent sinopulmonary infections despite completing posttransplant immunosuppression.</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>What explains the persistent hypogammaglobulinemia despite successful T-cell engraftment?</p></list-item><list-item><p>What additional treatment should be initiated?</p></list-item><list-item><p>How would you monitor for late complications posthematopoietic cell transplantation?</p></list-item><list-item><p>What is the expected long-term prognosis for this patient?</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Differential diagnoses should be considered when evaluating patients with clinical features of the following&#x000a0;X-linked immunodeficiencies:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>X-CGD:</bold> Consider cystic fibrosis, hyper-IgE syndrome, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency, glutathione synthetase deficiency, and Crohn disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>X-linked hyper-IgM:</bold> PI3K-delta syndrome, ataxia-telangiectasia, Nijmegen breakage syndrome, and common variable immunodeficiency (CVID)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>XLP:</bold> Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, CVID, Chediak-Higashi syndrome, Griscelli syndrome, and XIAP deficiency (XLP type 2)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>WAS:</bold> Omenn syndrome, IPEX, Netherton syndrome, hyper-IgE syndrome, DOCK8 deficiency, and atopic dermatitis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>X-SCID:</bold> JAK3 deficiency (also presenting with T-B+NK- phenotype) and other SCID subtypes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>XLA:</bold> Transient hypogammaglobulinemia of infancy, CVID, autosomal recessive agammaglobulinemia, and combined immunodeficiencies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>IPEX:</bold> IPEX-like syndromes, neonatal enteropathies, eosinophilic enteropathies, and other severe combined immunodeficiencies</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Prognosis across X-linked primary immunodeficiencies has improved significantly with earlier diagnosis and advances in antimicrobial prophylaxis, immunoglobulin replacement therapy, and supportive care. Long-term survival is strongly influenced by the timing of definitive therapy, with hematopoietic cell transplantation offering the best outcomes when performed before severe infections or organ damage occur.</p>
        <p>In patients with X-linked chronic granulomatous disease, life expectancy now exceeds 40 years with modern prophylactic antimicrobials and early hematopoietic cell transplantation, although mortality still largely results from severe fungal infections and inflammatory complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;However, individuals with X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome have a median survival of approximately 25 years with supportive care, as severe opportunistic infections, liver and biliary tract disease, and malignancies remain the leading causes of mortality, though long-term outcomes improve significantly with hematopoietic cell transplantation performed before liver disease develops.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Long-term mortality in individuals with XLP has been significantly reduced due to modern interventions, eg, rituximab, prophylactic immunoglobulin, and early hematopoietic cell transplantation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Similarly, outcomes for patients with WAS have improved dramatically with early hematopoietic cell transplantation and gene therapy, resulting in excellent long-term survival and quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Most patients with X-SCID achieve durable immune reconstitution and long-term survival when treated with appropriate hematopoietic cell transplantation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>Individuals with XLA typically survive into adulthood with good overall health when maintained on consistent immunoglobulin replacement therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r41">[41]</xref>&#x000a0;Hematopoietic cell transplantation provides a curative option for patients with IPEX syndrome, although residual endocrinopathies may persist due to prior organ damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r45">[45]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>X-linked primary immunodeficiencies are associated with significant infectious, inflammatory, autoimmune, and malignant complications that vary by disorder. For instance, X-CGD predisposes patients to recurrent abscesses, suppurative adenitis, osteomyelitis, pneumonia, and granulomatous or inflammatory bowel disease, with <italic toggle="yes">Aspergillus</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Burkholderia</italic> species causing particularly severe infections, while X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome commonly leads to bronchiectasis, sclerosing cholangitis, cryptosporidial cholangiopathy, and an increased risk of malignancy.</p>
        <p>Furthermore, XLP is associated with fulminant infectious mononucleosis, EBV-driven hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, lymphoma, lymphocytic vasculitis, and aplastic anemia.&#x000a0;WAS often results in EBV-associated lymphoma, severe autoimmune complications, and life-threatening hemorrhage due to thrombocytopenia.&#x000a0;X-SCID is uniformly fatal in infancy without treatment due to severe opportunistic infections.&#x000a0;XLA predisposes to chronic lung disease, bronchiectasis, and life-threatening enteroviral infections (eg, chronic meningoencephalitis).&#x000a0;IPEX syndrome causes progressive multiorgan autoimmune damage, including insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus, severe enteropathy with malabsorption, and autoimmune cytopenias.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Early genetic carrier screening in families with known X-linked immunodeficiencies is crucial for identifying at-risk individuals before symptomatic disease develops. Newborn screening programs for SCID enable presymptomatic diagnosis and treatment, significantly improving outcomes. Genetic counseling should be offered to all families with affected members to inform reproductive decision-making and enable early intervention strategies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r48">[48]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s13" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of a few X-linked immunodeficiencies requires high-end diagnostic studies, and clinical findings alone do not suffice to determine the changes in the affected organ system.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r49">[49]</xref></p>
        <p>WAS should be considered in all males with a very early age of symptom onset, decreased MPV, high eosinophil count, and high IgE levels, along with low CD+8 T lymphocyte count and high NK cell count, to differentiate it from ITP-related illness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31431.r27">[27]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>X-linked primary immunodeficiencies represent a group of severe inherited disorders that impair cellular and humoral immunity, leading to recurrent infections, immune dysregulation, and significant morbidity beginning in early life. Conditions such as X-CGD, X-HIGM, XLP, WAS, X-SCID, XLA, and IPEX share characteristic patterns of infection, autoimmunity, and lymphoid abnormalities that guide clinical suspicion. Early recognition supported by immunologic studies and genetic confirmation enables timely treatment, including antimicrobial prophylaxis, immunoglobulin replacement, immunomodulatory therapy, and hematopoietic cell transplantation, with gene-based therapies emerging as promising options.</p>
        <p>Optimal care requires coordinated efforts across the healthcare team. Physicians, general practitioners, and advanced practitioners must recognize early warning signs, initiate appropriate diagnostic testing, and collaborate closely with immunologists to guide definitive evaluation. Nurses support patient education, monitoring for complications, and adherence to prophylactic and replacement therapies. Pharmacists contribute expertise in antimicrobial stewardship, immunoglobulin management, and therapy safety. Integrated communication and shared decision-making across the care team enhance the timeliness of diagnosis, ensure continuity of treatment, and improve long-term patient-centered outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31431.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
